Myth: In the time before time, Silence ruled absolutely. No bird sang, no wind whispered, no water laughed over stones. The world existed in perfect, terrible quiet.

A young shepherd named Kael refused to accept this emptiness. He gathered fragments of everything that wished to speak—rustling leaves that longed to dance, stones that yearned to tumble, water that dreamed of rushing free. He wove these together not with thread, but with breath and intention, creating a tapestry of potential sound.

When his work was complete, Kael climbed to where earth met sky and released his creation with a shout—the first voice the world had ever known. The tapestry exploded outward, each fragment finding its voice. Leaves learned to rustle, stones to clatter, water to babble and roar.

But Kael's shout did not fade. It scattered into a thousand pieces, each becoming a different sound: laughter, music, thunder, whispers. These sounds settled into everything—into wind and wave, into bird and beast, into the hearts of all people yet to come.

From that day, weavers and singers have been kin, for both understand the same truth: that beauty must be released into the world, even if it means watching it transform into something beyond our control.
